This research examines the wellposedness of a family of novel generalizations to the KP-I equation, specifically equations of the form
\begin{align*}
\partial_t u&+L(a) u + u^{2}\partial_x^{a_2}(u\circ x^{-a_1})=0,
\end{align*}
where $a_2\ge 1$, $a_1>0$, and $a=1+\frac{2}{a_2}$. We demonstrate that for this equation family, which encompasses the KP-I equation as a special case, strong dispersion enables the use of almost conserved quantities to prove l^2 theory existence. Notably, our findings contrast with previous global wellposedness results for KP-I and its various generalizations, which were typically obtained through wave trace inequality methods. This paper's methods are distinct, relying instead on the L^2 theory combined with almost conserved quantity properties.
